>
  <p>
    Anyone can build a good-looking website. In Utah, the Division of Professional Licensing (DOPL) keeps the public record that
    tells you whether a construction company is licensed, whether that license is active, and whether it has been disciplined.
    Checking takes a few minutes and is the single best protection you have before signing a fence or wall contract.
  </p>

  <h2>Step by step: check a Utah contractor license</h2>
  <ol>
    {steps.map((s) => (
      <li><strong>{s.name}.</strong> {s.text}</li>
    ))}
  </ol>
  <p>
    The lookup is at <a href={lookup} rel="noopener">secure.utah.gov/llv</a>. When we checked it on September 26, 2026, it showed data
    current as of September 25, 2026. DOPL also publishes
    <a href="https://commerce.utah.gov/dopl/enforcement/disciplinary-actions-and-citations/" rel="noopener">disciplinary actions and citations</a> if you
    want to read the detail behind a flag on a record.
  </p>

  <h2>What a license record shows</h2>
  <p>
    A DOPL contractor record lists the profession and license type, the license number, the status, the issue and expiration dates,
    any agency or disciplinary action, and the license's classifications with the qualifier named for each. The classification is the
    part most homeowners skip: it describes the kinds of work the license covers. Ask the company which of its classifications covers
    your fence, block wall, or retaining wall, and check that it is on the record.
  </p>
  <p>
    For fences, DOPL's current contractor rule (<a href="https://adminrules.utah.gov/public/rule/R156-55a/Current%20Rules" rel="noopener">Utah Admin. Code R156-55a</a>,
    effective August 7, 2026) lists fences and guardrails, and retaining walls, within the scope of the S330 Landscape and Recreation
    Contractor classification. The same rule lists brick, block, and concrete blocks within the scope of the S230 Masonry, Siding,
    Stucco, Glass, and Rain Gutter Contractor classification, which DOPL records show as "S230 - Masonry, S/S, Glass, Rain Gutter."
    Broader classifications and exceptions exist, so for a block wall or a retaining wall that holds up a
    driveway, structure, or slope, ask the company which classification it works under and confirm it with DOPL.
  </p>

  <h2>Insurance</h2>
  <p>
    Utah Code §58-55-302 requires a license applicant to file proof of public liability insurance, and of workers compensation
    insurance covering employees, before DOPL issues the license. That is a snapshot at licensing. Ask for a current certificate of
    insurance for the job, and check the policy dates.
  </p>

  <h2>Small jobs and the exemption question</h2>
  <p>
    Utah's licensing law exempts some small jobs. Under Utah Code §58-55-305, a person doing alteration, repair, remodeling, addition,
    or improvement work on a building with a contracted value under {ex.under}, including labor and materials, is exempt from licensing,
    and one whose project is over {ex.affirmationOver} must file a one-time affirmation with DOPL that they carry liability insurance (and workers
    compensation if applicable). This is the version in effect as of September 2026.
  </p>
  <p>
    Whether a particular fence or wall job fits that exemption is DOPL's call, not ours. If a company tells you it does not need a
    license, ask which exemption it is relying on, and check with DOPL before you sign.
  </p>

  <h2>A protection that depends on hiring licensed</h2>
  <p>
    Utah generally protects a homeowner who has a written contract with a licensed or exempt contractor and pays that contractor in
    full: unpaid subcontractors or suppliers cannot keep a lien on the home and may instead claim from the state's Residence Lien
    Recovery Fund (Utah Code §38-11-107 and §38-11-204). It is one more reason to put the contract in writing and to hire a company
    whose license you have checked.
  </p>

  <h2>How to compare fence and wall quotes</h2>
  <p>Put the quotes side by side on the same six points:</p>
  <IconList items={compare} cols={2} />

  <h2>Red flags</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>No license number, or a name that does not match anything in the DOPL lookup.</li>
    <li>"We never need a permit" without asking where your front setback is or how tall the retaining wall will be.</li>
    <li>A large cash deposit before any materials are ordered.</li>
    <li>No written scope, or a price that only appears after the old fence is removed.</li>
    <li>Pressure to sign the same day.</li>
  </ul>
